many of us have been living with vaccine  mandates for months and they're not  going anywhere anytime soon the  mandate's kicking in across the country  starting today you'll need one of these  a vaccine card people will soon be  required to show proof of full  vaccination to do most indoor activity  so far most vaccine orders issued by  cities and states have been upheld with  judges citing a supreme court precedent  set more than a century ago when it  ruled on a state's smallpox vaccine  mandate but now cases involving the  federal government's power to mandate  vaccines are before the supreme court  and they could ultimately help decide  who has the power to order shots in arms  so here's what you need to know about  the legal precedent for vaccine mandates  in america and what the supreme court is  looking at now  back in 1905 the issue of vaccine  mandates reached the supreme court when  a lutheran pastor named henning jacobson  refused to get a government-mandated  smallpox vaccine in part because he said  he had suffered seriously from previous  vaccination he sued the state claiming  that the vaccine was dangerous and that  healthy law-abiding people like himself  didn't pose a danger to the community  but the supreme court ruled against him  writing for the majority justice john  marshall harlan stated the liberty  secured by the constitution does not  import an absolute right in each person  to be wholly freed from restraint there  are manifold restraints to which every  person is necessarily subject for the  common good in other words personal  freedom can be limited particularly when  it threatens the community since then  authorities have long implemented public  health mandates to try to keep  communities safe fast forward to today  and the coronavirus pandemic since  coveted vaccines became widely available  last year more than 20 states and dozens  of cities have adopted vaccine mandates  and those mandates have largely been  upheld by judges appointed by  republicans and democrats alike many  citing jacobson the federal government  has also ordered vaccines for a wide  range of people like federal workers and  health care workers who treat patients  on medicare and medicaid and through the  occupational safety and health  administration or osha the  administration ordered all businesses  with more than 100 employees to ensure  that their workers are either vaccinated  or tested weekly we've been patient  but our patients is wearing thin  with the federal government taking an  assertive role dozens of states business  groups and religious groups sued to  block the mandates they argue that osha  which is tasked with ensuring safe  working conditions and the centers for  medicare and medicaid services which  oversees government health insurance  programs don't have the authority to  issue such mandates under federal law  with vaccination deadlines looming for  businesses and health care workers and  with legal challenges mounting the  supreme court agreed to weigh in with  fast-track arguments we will hear  argument first this morning in  application  21a 244. the liberal justices appeared  to support the government's order and at  one point there was a back and forth  about the state's power to police public  health power that was affirmed by the  jacobson case if it's within the police  power to protect the health and welfare  of workers  you seem to be saying the states can do  it but you're saying the federal  government can't  even though it's facing  the same crisis in interstate commerce  that states are facing within their own  borders  i am not sure i understand the  distinction the federal government has  no police power oh it does have power  with respect to protecting the health  and safety  of workers but many of the conservative  justices expressed skepticism about the  federal government's power it seems to  me that the more and more  mandates that pop up in different  agencies it's fair i wonder if it's not  fair for us to look at the court as a  general exercise of power by the federal  government and then ask the questions of  well why isn't congress have a say in  this and why don't this why doesn't this  be the primary responsibility of the  states the court is expected to come to  a decision on whether the biden mandates  can be enforced in the coming weeks to  understand what that could mean for  millions of americans let's call my  colleague brent kendall he covers the  supreme court for wsj hi shelby hey  brent he says that even though the cases  are being heard on a fast-track basis  the supreme court's decisions could have  lasting implications for now the court  doesn't even have to say with finality  whether what the biden administration is  doing is is lawful or not it just has to  decide if it's likely lawful okay so  even though they're not ruling on the  merits necessarily it will determine the  fate of the mandates it will i mean the  merits is part of this consideration  even though they don't have to issue a  full merits ruling if they don't want to  i think it's likely for all practical  purposes that what the supreme court  says here will end this some law  professors point out that the  conservative leaning court has not been  a fan of expansive federal regulation  instead preferring to defer to the  states for example it blocked the biden  administration's eviction moratorium in  august in our constitutional system the  way things are set up is that you know  the federal government has limited power  so states are typically the first line  of defense for deciding how best to  protect health within their borders  brent says a big question is whether the  federal government can overrule states  particularly those that don't want to  implement the vaccine mandates the  supreme court's ruling on the biden  mandates might decide the fate of the  mandates themselves but it's unlikely  it'll move the needle much in terms of  the public debate over vaccines after  all jacobson lost his case more than a  century ago yet many still hold his same  beliefs about individual liberties and  the right to remain unvaccinated so keep  your eye on wsj.com for news of the  court's ruling and if you thought this  video was interesting be sure to  subscribe to the channel so you can  follow me as i cover more news that's  impacting our lives see you next time  [Music]
 

